<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Martinuzzi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1482">1482</a>–<a href="/years/1551">1551</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as George Utješenović, Frater Georgius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George Martinuzzi was a Croatian-born Pauline monk and statesman who served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-zapolya">John Zápolya</a> as treasurer and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Várad. After Zápolya's death in 1540 he governed the eastern Hungarian lands for the infant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-sigismund-zapolya">John Sigismund</a>, balancing between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. In 1551 he arranged the transfer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> to Ferdinand I and was made a cardinal, but was murdered that December by Habsburg officers who suspected him of dealings with the Ottomans.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-martinuzzi.jpg" alt="George Martinuzzi" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fr%C3%A1ter_Gy%C3%B6rgy_MTA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born Juraj Utješenović at Kamičak Castle near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skradin">Skradin</a> on 18 June 1482, he was the youngest son of the Croatian lesser nobleman Grgur Utješenović and Anna Martinuzzi, of a Venetian patrician family. Most details of his life before 1528 are uncertain. His father and two eldest brothers died while he was a child, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took the family estates. From 1490 he was raised at the castle of Hunyad by Duke John Corvinus, and around 1503 he moved to the court of Hedwig of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cieszyn">Cieszyn</a>, widow of Count Stephen Zápolya, as a page and then a guard. At 24 he entered the Pauline Order, learnt to read and write, and studied at Jasna Góra in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czestochowa">Częstochowa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs">Battle of Mohács</a> in 1526 Hedwig's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-zapolya">John Zápolya</a> was elected king, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-holy-roman-emperor">Ferdinand of Habsburg</a> claimed the throne. When John fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> after his defeat at Szina in 1528, Martinuzzi went with him and served as his envoy to his supporters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. In 1532 Alvise Gritti made him provisor of Buda Castle. He centralised the royal revenues, checked the tax collectors and drew income from trade in leather, fleece, wine and grain, which earned him charges of greed. He led the secret talks that produced the Treaty of Várad of 24 February 1538, by which Hungary was to be reunited when one of the two kings died, and urged John to marry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-jagiellon">Isabella Jagiellon</a>.</p><p>After John's death on 22 July 1540 he had the Diet elect the infant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-sigismund-zapolya">John Sigismund</a> king. He defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> against Ferdinand's siege in 1541 and called on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> then seized Buda. The Treaty of Gyalu of 29 December 1541 left the eastern lands to John Sigismund, and Martinuzzi tried to keep the state intact by friendly relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> without offending the Ottomans. In 1550 he imprisoned Queen Isabella at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alba-iulia">Gyulafehérvár</a>, drove out the hospodars of Moldavia and Wallachia and beat the Turks at Déva. The Diet of Kolozsvár confirmed the agreement with Ferdinand in August 1551, and on 12 October Pope Julius III made him a cardinal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When he tried to mediate privately with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> after their capture of Csanád, the imperial general Giambattista Castaldo accused him of treason. On 16 December 1551 at Alvinc Castle his secretary Marco Aurelio Ferrari stabbed him from behind as he read a letter, and he was finished off by Sforza-Pallavicini and hired men. The pope excommunicated Ferdinand and his generals, but lifted the sentence in 1555 after hearing 116 witnesses.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “George Martinuzzi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Zápolya's death in 1540, his widow Isabella Jagiellon and the bishop <b>George Martinuzzi</b> governed for the infant John Sigismund.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-hungarian-kingdom">Eastern Hungarian Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kamičac, in what is now <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>. Died in Vințu de Jos, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.89,15.98&amp;year=1526" aria-label="Kamičac on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of George Martinuzzi"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4144 3771h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4144 3771h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.89,15.98&amp;year=1526">What was here in 1526? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
